---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { withBase } from '../lib/url';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Learning Roadmap for Shopify App Development — Shopify App Handbook"
  description="Everything free, step by step: three tracks — building real embedded Shopify apps on the React Router 7 stack, a general backend foundation underneath it, and a dedicated track for reading the config files, JSDoc, and build tooling that most tutorials skip and most developers get stuck on."
  accent="gold"
  footerNote="Free the whole way through: every tool and resource linked here has a genuine free tier as of September 2026. Hosting free tiers change often (Railway and Fly.io both dropped theirs) — re-check a platform's pricing page before relying on it long-term."
>
  <header>
    <p class="eyebrow">Full curriculum</p>
    <h1 class="page-title">Learning Roadmap for Shopify App Development</h1>
    <p class="lede">The full reference for going deeper: three tracks — building real embedded Shopify apps on the React Router 7 stack, a general backend foundation underneath it, and a dedicated track for reading the config files, JSDoc, and build tooling that most tutorials skip and most developers get stuck on.</p>
    <p class="lede-note">New here? The <a href={withBase("/")}>homepage</a> walks the whole site in order. This page is what you come back to once you've built something and want to go deeper on a specific module — read modules in any order you need, not necessarily top to bottom.</p>
    <div class="meta-row">
      <span class="meta-chip">3 tracks</span>
      <span class="meta-chip">20 modules</span>
      <span class="meta-chip">~4–5 months part-time</span>
      <span class="meta-chip">$0 required</span>
    </div>
  </header>

  <details class="setup" id="setup" open>
    <summary>Before you start — accounts &amp; tools (all free, no card needed unless noted)</summary>
    <div class="setup-grid">
      <div class="setup-card" data-tone="gold">
        <h4>Local machine</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="https://nodejs.org/en/download" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Node.js LTS</a> — install via <a href="https://github.com/nvm-sh/nvm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">nvm</a> (or <a href="https://github.com/coreybutler/nvm-windows" target="_blank" rel="noopener">nvm-windows</a>) so you can switch versions. <span class="why">This is the JavaScript runtime everything else runs on — the CLI, your app's server, and its build tools all need it installed first.</span></li>
          <li><a href="https://code.visualstudio.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">VS Code</a> + extensions: ESLint, Prisma, GraphQL: Language Feature Support. <span class="why">Not required, but these three give you real-time type errors and autocomplete for the exact files this stack uses most.</span></li>
          <li><a href="https://git-scm.com/downloads" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Git</a>. <span class="why">Version control — the Shopify CLI sets up a repo for you when you scaffold an app, but Git itself has to already be installed for that to work, and you'll need it again to deploy.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="setup-card" data-tone="violet">
        <h4>Shopify side</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="https://www.shopify.com/partners" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Shopify Partner account</a> — free, no store purchase needed. <span class="why">Your developer identity on Shopify: it's how Shopify knows which apps and stores belong to you, and it's where development stores and app records live.</span></li>
          <li>A free <a href="https://shopify.dev/docs/apps/tools/development-stores" target="_blank" rel="noopener">development store</a>, created from the Partner dashboard. <span class="why">A real, fully-working store that only you can see — for installing and testing your app safely. It's separate from any live store you'd actually sell through.</span></li>
          <li><a href="https://shopify.dev/docs/apps/build/scaffold-app" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Shopify CLI</a> — installed per-project via <code>npm</code>, not globally. <span class="why">The one tool this entire workflow runs through: it scaffolds new apps, runs a local dev tunnel so Shopify's servers can reach your machine, generates extensions, and deploys.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="setup-card" data-tone="teal">
        <h4>Free hosted database (pick one)</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="https://neon.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Neon</a> — free Postgres, permanent plan, no card. <span class="why">You'll swap this in for the CLI's local SQLite database once you're ready to deploy for real — Prisma just points at a different connection string, no code changes.</span></li>
          <li><a href="https://supabase.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Supabase</a> — free Postgres + extras, no card. <span class="why">Same idea as Neon, with auth/storage extras bundled in that you likely won't need for your first app, but are there if you do.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="setup-card" data-tone="blue">
        <h4>Free hosting (pick one, for later)</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="https://vercel.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vercel</a> — Hobby plan, free forever for personal projects. <span class="why">Where your app's server actually runs once it's live, as opposed to just running on your own machine during development.</span></li>
          <li><a href="https://render.com/docs/free" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Render</a> — free web service (spins down when idle). <span class="why">An alternative to Vercel; the free tier sleeps after inactivity, so the first request after a quiet period is noticeably slow.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="setup-note">
      Heads up on hosting churn: Railway and Fly.io used to have real free tiers — both now only give you a one-time trial credit and require a card for anything beyond that, so they're left off this roadmap's "free" recommendations. Render's own free Postgres database expires 30 days after creation, which is why Neon/Supabase (permanent free tier) are listed separately as the database, with Render/Vercel just running the app. This changes over time, so re-check pricing pages before committing.
    </div>
  </details>

  <p class="howto">Every module below expands the same way: <b>bold bullets</b> are the concepts to actually understand, and the collapsible sections underneath break down <b>Tools you'll use</b>, a <b>Step-by-step path</b> to learn it, <b>Free resources</b>, <b>Where people get lost</b> (the specific confusions this roadmap exists to prevent), and a <b>Checkpoint project</b> to prove it stuck. Do modules in order within a track — each one assumes the last.</p>

  <div class="tabs">
    <input type="radio" name="track-tab" id="tab-shopify" checked>
    <input type="radio" name="track-tab" id="tab-backend">
    <input type="radio" name="track-tab" id="tab-tools">

    <div class="tab-labels">
      <label for="tab-shopify">Shopify App Development</label>
      <label for="tab-backend">Backend Development</label>
      <label for="tab-tools">Toolchain &amp; Code Literacy</label>
    </div>

    <div class="panels">

      <!-- ============================================================ -->
      <!-- SHOPIFY TRACK -->
      <!-- ============================================================ -->
      <section id="panel-shopify" class="track shopify">
        <p class="track-intro">From React fundamentals to a real, installable embedded app: React → TypeScript → GraphQL → the React Router 7 app template → auth → Prisma → webhooks/billing → extensions → testing → shipping.</p>

        <nav class="quicknav">
          <a href="#s-react">React</a>
          <a href="#s-ts">TypeScript</a>
          <a href="#s-graphql">GraphQL + Admin API</a>
          <a href="#s-rr7">Node + React Router 7</a>
          <a href="#s-auth">OAuth &amp; Sessions</a>
          <a href="#s-prisma">Prisma + DB</a>
          <a href="#s-webhooks">Webhooks, Billing, Jobs</a>
          <a href="#s-ext">Extensions</a>
          <a href="#s-test">Testing</a>
          <a href="#s-deploy">Shipping</a>
        </nav>

        <div class="rail">

          <div class="node" id="s-react">
            <div class="card">
              <h3>React Fundamentals</h3>
              <p class="tagline">Foundation for Polaris, App Bridge, and every extension surface.</p>
              <ul>
                <li>Components, JSX, props, composition and children</li>
                <li>The render cycle: what triggers a re-render, why it matters for performance</li>
                <li>Side effects with <code>useEffect</code> — and the discipline of avoiding it when you don't need it</li>
                <li>Context, custom hooks, controlled forms and lists with keys</li>
              </ul>

              <details>
                <summary>Concepts to master: State Management (worked example)</summary>
                <div class="path">
                  <p><b>Fundamentals to understand first:</b> a component re-renders when its state or props change; state is just "data that changes over time and the UI needs to react to." Get comfortable with <code>useState</code> updater functions, why objects/arrays need to be replaced not mutated, and why two renders with the same array contents can still be "different" (referential equality) — this single idea explains half of React's confusing bugs.</p>
                  <p><b>The layers of state, cheapest first:</b></p>
                  <ol class="path">
                    <li><b>Local component state</b> — <code>useState</code>. Use for anything only one component (and its direct children) cares about: an input value, a toggle, a modal's open/closed flag.</li>
                    <li><b>Lifted state</b> — move state up to the nearest common parent when two sibling components need to share it. This is the default fix before reaching for anything fancier.</li>
                    <li><b>Server state</b> — in a Shopify app on React Router 7, most of your "state" is actually data fetched from Shopify or your database via <code>loader</code>/<code>action</code> functions, not client state at all. This is the biggest mental shift from generic React tutorials.</li>
                    <li><b>URL state</b> — filters, pagination, selected tab: put it in the URL (search params) so it survives refresh and is shareable, instead of a <code>useState</code>.</li>
                    <li><b>Complex local state</b> — <code>useReducer</code> when a component has several state values that change together (e.g. a multi-step form).</li>
                    <li><b>Global/shared client state</b> — Context for rarely-changing app-wide values (theme, locale). For anything that changes often, prefer server state or a small library — Context re-renders every consumer on every change, which is a common performance trap.</li>
                  </ol>
                  <p><b>Tools mostly used in real Shopify app dev:</b> <code>useState</code>/<code>useReducer</code> (built in), React Router's own loaders/actions/<code>useFetcher</code> as the primary "data layer" (built in — no extra library needed), and <a href="https://tanstack.com/query/latest" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TanStack Query</a> only if you go further client-driven than the template defaults. Redux/Zustand/Jotai are common in general React jobs but rarely needed for embedded Shopify apps — the app template's loader/action model already replaces most of what a global store would do.</p>
                </div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Tools you'll actually use</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li>React 18/19 — hooks API, no class components in new code</li>
                  <li>React DevTools browser extension — inspect props/state/re-renders live</li>
                  <li>Vite (dev server, see the Toolchain track) rather than Create React App, which is discontinued</li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Step-by-step path</summary>
                <ol class="path">
                  <li>Work through the official interactive tutorial end to end, typing every example yourself rather than reading.</li>
                  <li>Build a tic-tac-toe or todo app with only <code>useState</code> — no libraries.</li>
                  <li>Add <code>fetch</code> against a public JSON API (e.g. a fake products endpoint) and practice loading/error states by hand.</li>
                  <li>Refactor a shared toggle/filter between two sibling components by lifting state up.</li>
                  <li>Rebuild the same filter using URL search params instead of <code>useState</code>, so it survives a page refresh.</li>
                </ol>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Free resources</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://react.dev/learn" target="_blank" rel="noopener">react.dev — Learn React</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://epicreact.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Epic React by Kent C. Dodds</a> <span class="free-tag">some free content</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://www.freecodecamp.org/news/tag/react/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">freeCodeCamp — React articles &amp; course</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Where people get lost</summary>
                <ul class="pitfalls">
                  <li><b>Mutating state directly</b> (<code>state.push(...)</code> then calling <code>setState(state)</code>) looks like it works, then silently breaks re-renders elsewhere. Always create a new array/object.</li>
                  <li><b>useEffect overuse</b> — reaching for an effect to "sync" derived data that could just be calculated during render. If you can compute it from existing props/state without a side effect, don't use <code>useEffect</code>.</li>
                  <li><b>Confusing props with state</b> — props flow down and are read-only to the child; if you find yourself wanting to "set a prop," you actually want lifted state and a callback prop instead.</li>
                </ul>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Checkpoint project</summary>
                <p class="checkpoint">Build a standalone React app (e.g. a product filter/search UI on dummy JSON) with no backend yet — filters live in the URL, list state is local, and nothing mutates state directly.</p>
              </details>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="node" id="s-ts">
            <div class="card">
              <h3>TypeScript</h3>
              <p class="tagline">The app template is TypeScript-first throughout — loaders, actions, GraphQL responses, Prisma models, all typed.</p>
              <ul>
                <li>Basic types, interfaces vs. <code>type</code>, unions and narrowing</li>
                <li>Typing component props, function signatures, and event handlers</li>
                <li>Generics — enough to <i>read</i> library types, not necessarily author complex ones</li>
                <li>Typing async functions, <code>fetch</code>/GraphQL responses, and error handling with <code>unknown</code></li>
              </ul>

              <details>
                <summary>Tools you'll actually use</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><code>tsc</code> — the compiler/type-checker itself (see the Toolchain track for <code>tsconfig.json</code>)</li>
                  <li>VS Code's built-in TypeScript language server — inline errors and autocomplete, no extension needed</li>
                  <li>Generated types from Shopify's GraphQL schema (via the CLI's codegen) and from Prisma's schema — you'll read far more generated types than you write by hand</li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Step-by-step path</summary>
                <ol class="path">
                  <li>Go through the Handbook sections on basic types, narrowing, and interfaces vs. types.</li>
                  <li>Convert the Module 1 React app to TypeScript — type props, state, and fetch responses.</li>
                  <li>Deliberately break a type (wrong prop type, wrong return type) and read the compiler error until you understand exactly what it's telling you — the errors are the whole point.</li>
                  <li>Learn just enough generics to read <code>Array&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>Promise&lt;T&gt;</code>, and one library's generic hook signature (e.g. <code>useState&lt;T&gt;</code>).</li>
                </ol>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Free resources</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/intro.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TypeScript Handbook</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://www.totaltypescript.com/tutorials/beginners-typescript" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Total TypeScript — Beginner's Tutorial</a> <span class="free-tag">free tier</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://www.typescriptlang.org/play" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TypeScript Playground</a> — test snippets instantly <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Where people get lost</summary>
                <ul class="pitfalls">
                  <li><b>Reaching for <code>any</code> to make an error go away</b> — it silences the checker instead of fixing the mismatch, and the error usually comes back worse two files later. Use <code>unknown</code> plus a narrowing check when you genuinely don't know the shape yet.</li>
                  <li><b>Type vs. value confusion</b> — an <code>interface</code>/<code>type</code> disappears at runtime (it's compiled away); it can't be used where a real value is expected (e.g. in a condition or as an object you pass around).</li>
                  <li><b>Red squiggles that aren't actually your bug</b> — a type error three files upstream (a wrong return type in a Prisma query, say) shows up as an error where you're using the result. Read the error's file/line, not just where VS Code is scrolled to.</li>
                </ul>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Checkpoint project</summary>
                <p class="checkpoint">The converted Module 1 app compiles with zero <code>any</code> and zero <code>@ts-ignore</code>, and every fetch response has an explicit type.</p>
              </details>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="node" id="s-graphql">
            <div class="card">
              <h3>GraphQL + Shopify Admin API</h3>
              <p class="tagline">Shopify's Admin API is GraphQL-only going forward — the REST Admin API is legacy.</p>
              <ul>
                <li>Queries, mutations, variables, fragments, aliases</li>
                <li>Reading a schema in GraphiQL: required vs. optional fields, connection/edge/node pagination shape</li>
                <li>Cursor-based pagination (<code>first</code>/<code>after</code>, <code>pageInfo.hasNextPage</code>)</li>
                <li>Cost-based rate limiting and when to reach for Bulk Operations instead of paginating by hand</li>
              </ul>

              <details>
                <summary>Tools you'll actually use</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li>Shopify GraphiQL app (in-browser explorer against your own dev store) — the fastest way to test a query before writing code</li>
                  <li><code>@shopify/graphql-client</code> / the authenticated <code>admin.graphql()</code> helper the app template gives you inside loaders and actions</li>
                  <li>GraphQL codegen (via Shopify CLI) — generates TypeScript types straight from the schema and your <code>.graphql</code> queries, so you're not hand-typing API responses</li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Step-by-step path</summary>
                <ol class="path">
                  <li>Work through a general GraphQL primer (queries/mutations/variables) with any public GraphQL playground.</li>
                  <li>Open GraphiQL against your dev store and query <code>products</code>, then add fields, then paginate with <code>first</code>/<code>after</code>.</li>
                  <li>Write a mutation (e.g. <code>productUpdate</code>) in GraphiQL and confirm the change lands in the store admin.</li>
                  <li>Move that same query/mutation into the app template's <code>loader</code>/<code>action</code> using the authenticated <code>admin.graphql()</code> client.</li>
                </ol>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Free resources</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://www.howtographql.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">How to GraphQL</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://shopify.dev/docs/api/admin-graphql" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Shopify Admin API (GraphQL) docs</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://shopify.dev/docs/api/usage/bulk-operations/queries" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bulk Operations guide</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Where people get lost</summary>
                <ul class="pitfalls">
                  <li><b>The connection/edge/node shape</b> — a list of products isn't just an array, it's <code>products.edges[].node</code> plus a separate <code>pageInfo</code>. This trips up almost everyone the first time.</li>
                  <li><b>Cost errors under load</b> — a query that works fine once can hit <code>THROTTLED</code> under real usage; you have to read the <code>extensions.cost</code> data in the response, not just the error message.</li>
                  <li><b>REST muscle memory</b> — there's no separate "endpoint per resource"; everything goes through one GraphQL endpoint, and the query itself decides what comes back.</li>
                </ul>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Checkpoint project</summary>
                <p class="checkpoint">In GraphiQL against a dev store, query products with pagination, then write a mutation to update a product field, then reproduce both from inside the app template's loader/action.</p>
              </details>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="node" id="s-rr7">
            <div class="card">
              <h3>Node.js + React Router 7 (the app template)</h3>
              <p class="tagline">This is the actual Shopify app template — where everything above converges into one real app.</p>
              <ul>
                <li>Node basics: modules (ESM), <code>async</code>/<code>await</code>, environment variables, the request/response model</li>
                <li>React Router 7's file-based routes, <code>loader</code> (reads, runs on the server) and <code>action</code> (writes, runs on the server) functions</li>
                <li>App Bridge React — how your app gets embedded inside Shopify Admin, session-token auth, navigation</li>
                <li>Polaris Web Components — Shopify's UI kit, so your app visually matches Admin</li>
              </ul>

              <details>
                <summary>Tools you'll actually use</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><code>@shopify/shopify-app-react-router</code> — the package that wires auth, sessions, and webhooks into the template</li>
                  <li><code>@shopify/polaris</code> and <code>@shopify/app-bridge-react</code> — UI and embedding</li>
                  <li>Shopify CLI's <code>dev</code> command — starts your app, tunnels it to a public URL, and updates your app's config on Shopify automatically</li>
                  <li>React Router's <code>useLoaderData</code>, <code>useActionData</code>, <code>useFetcher</code>, <code>Form</code> — the actual "data layer" you'll use constantly</li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Step-by-step path</summary>
                <ol class="path">
                  <li>Scaffold the template with the Shopify CLI and run it against your dev store — get "app installed and loads a blank page" before writing anything.</li>
                  <li>Add one route that loads real product data via a <code>loader</code> and renders it in Polaris components.</li>
                  <li>Add a form that submits to an <code>action</code> and mutates something (e.g. a product title) — confirm optimistic UI and error states.</li>
                  <li>Read the generated <code>app/shopify.server.ts</code> (or equivalent) end to end so the "magic" auth setup isn't a black box.</li>
                  <li>Add a second, nested route and understand how layouts and loaders compose down the route tree.</li>
                </ol>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Free resources</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://github.com/Shopify/shopify-app-template-react-router" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Shopify App Template (React Router)</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://shopify.dev/docs/apps/build/build?framework=reactRouter" target="_blank" rel="noopener">shopify.dev — Build a Shopify app (React Router)</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://shopify.dev/docs/api/shopify-app-react-router/latest" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Shopify App package for React Router — API reference</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://reactrouter.com/en/main/route/loader" target="_blank" rel="noopener">React Router — Data loading docs</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://polaris.shopify.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Polaris component library</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Where people get lost</summary>
                <ul class="pitfalls">
                  <li><b>Loaders/actions run on the server, not the browser</b> — you can safely use secrets and direct database calls inside them, but any <code>console.log</code> there shows up in your terminal, not the browser console. This single fact confuses almost everyone coming from plain React.</li>
                  <li><b>The generated boilerplate looks scary before it looks obvious</b> — <code>shopify.server.ts</code>, <code>entry.server.tsx</code>, and route config files are all doing real, specific jobs (see the Toolchain track for reading unfamiliar files systematically instead of skimming and panicking).</li>
                  <li><b>Embedded vs. standalone confusion</b> — the app runs inside an iframe in Shopify Admin using session tokens, not cookies, for auth; treating it like a normal standalone web app breaks the embedding.</li>
                </ul>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Checkpoint project</summary>
                <p class="checkpoint">Scaffold the app template, run it on a dev store, and build a page that loads products via a loader into Polaris components, with an action that edits one field.</p>
              </details>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="node" id="s-auth">
            <div class="card">
              <h3>Authentication: OAuth &amp; Session Tokens</h3>
              <p class="tagline">The part everyone hand-waves — worth understanding directly since the template does it for you.</p>
              <ul>
                <li>Why apps need OAuth: your app never sees the merchant's password, only a scoped access token</li>
                <li>The install flow: redirect → merchant approves scopes → callback → access token stored per shop</li>
                <li>Session tokens (JWT) for embedded requests vs. the OAuth access token used to call the Admin API</li>
                <li>Scopes — requesting the minimum your app needs, and what happens when a merchant revokes access</li>
              </ul>

              <details>
                <summary>Tools you'll actually use</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li>The template's built-in <code>authenticate.admin(request)</code> helper — handles session-token verification for you on every loader/action</li>
                  <li>Prisma-backed session storage (see the Prisma module below) — where access tokens actually live</li>
                  <li>jwt.io (just to <i>look at</i> a decoded session token while learning — never paste real production tokens into third-party sites)</li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Step-by-step path</summary>
                <ol class="path">
                  <li>Read Shopify's OAuth overview once, fully, before touching code — the flow diagram is the whole mental model.</li>
                  <li>Install your dev app on the dev store and watch the network tab during install to see the redirect/callback happen.</li>
                  <li>Open a loader in the template and trace exactly what <code>authenticate.admin(request)</code> returns and where the access token comes from.</li>
                  <li>Uninstall and reinstall the app, and confirm you understand what gets cleaned up (the session row) vs. what doesn't.</li>
                </ol>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Free resources</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://shopify.dev/docs/apps/build/authentication-authorization" target="_blank" rel="noopener">shopify.dev — Authentication and authorization</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://shopify.dev/docs/apps/build/authentication-authorization/session-tokens" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Session tokens explained</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://jwt.io/introduction" target="_blank" rel="noopener">jwt.io — Introduction to JWTs</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Where people get lost</summary>
                <ul class="pitfalls">
                  <li><b>Session tokens and access tokens are not the same thing</b> — the session token proves the embedded request came from a logged-in merchant right now; the access token is what your server uses to call the Admin API on their behalf. Confusing the two is the single most common auth bug.</li>
                  <li><b>"It works locally, breaks after redeploy"</b> — usually a wrong <code>SHOPIFY_APP_URL</code>/redirect URL mismatch between your hosting and your app's config in the Partner dashboard.</li>
                  <li><b>Treating scopes as a formality</b> — requesting broad scopes "just in case" both slows app review and increases what a compromised token can do.</li>
                </ul>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Checkpoint project</summary>
                <p class="checkpoint">Explain out loud (or in a written note to yourself) the full request path from "merchant clicks a button in your embedded app" to "your server calls the Admin API" — naming every token involved and where it's verified.</p>
              </details>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="node" id="s-prisma">
            <div class="card">
              <h3>Prisma + Database</h3>
              <p class="tagline">For persisting anything beyond the current request — sessions, settings, custom data.</p>
              <ul>
                <li>Prisma schema syntax and migrations (<code>prisma migrate dev</code>)</li>
                <li>Prisma Client calls from inside loaders/actions</li>
                <li>SQLite (what the template ships with for local dev) vs. hosted Postgres (Neon/Supabase) for anything real</li>
                <li>How the template already uses Prisma for session storage — read that model before adding your own</li>
              </ul>

              <details>
                <summary>Tools you'll actually use</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li>Prisma CLI (<code>prisma migrate dev</code>, <code>prisma studio</code>, <code>prisma generate</code>)</li>
                  <li>Prisma Studio — a free local GUI to browse/edit your database's rows without writing SQL</li>
                  <li>Neon or Supabase as the production Postgres, swapped in via the <code>DATABASE_URL</code> env var</li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Step-by-step path</summary>
                <ol class="path">
                  <li>Read the template's existing <code>schema.prisma</code> — specifically the <code>Session</code> model — before writing your own.</li>
                  <li>Add a new model (e.g. merchant preferences), run <code>prisma migrate dev</code>, and inspect the generated migration SQL.</li>
                  <li>Read and write that model from a loader and an action.</li>
                  <li>Swap the local SQLite connection string for a free Neon/Supabase Postgres URL and re-run migrations against it.</li>
                  <li>Open Prisma Studio against both databases to confirm the data actually landed.</li>
                </ol>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Free resources</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://www.prisma.io/docs/getting-started" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Prisma — Getting Started</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://www.prisma.io/docs/orm/prisma-schema" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Prisma schema reference</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://neon.com/docs/guides/prisma" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Neon + Prisma guide</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Where people get lost</summary>
                <ul class="pitfalls">
                  <li><b>Forgetting to run <code>prisma generate</code></b> after editing the schema — the TypeScript types for Prisma Client are generated code, so edits to <code>schema.prisma</code> don't type-check anywhere until you regenerate.</li>
                  <li><b>SQLite locally, Postgres in production, and forgetting they diverge</b> — some field types and migrations behave slightly differently; test against Postgres before you ship.</li>
                  <li><b>Losing local data on every schema reset</b> — <code>migrate reset</code> wipes your local dev database; that's expected, not a bug.</li>
                </ul>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Checkpoint project</summary>
                <p class="checkpoint">Add a model, migrate it locally and against a free hosted Postgres, and read/write it from a loader and action — verified by looking at the rows in Prisma Studio.</p>
              </details>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="node" id="s-webhooks">
            <div class="card">
              <h3>Webhooks, Billing API &amp; Background Jobs</h3>
              <p class="tagline">How your app reacts to store events, and how it charges money.</p>
              <ul>
                <li>Shopify-managed webhooks: registering topics, verifying the HMAC signature, handling retries/duplicates</li>
                <li>Mandatory compliance webhooks (customer data request/redact, shop redact) — required for App Store approval</li>
                <li>The Billing API — recurring charges, usage-based charges, and the required test/dev-store billing mode</li>
                <li>Why long-running work (bulk data sync, emails) belongs in a background job, not inside the webhook handler itself</li>
              </ul>

              <details>
                <summary>Tools you'll actually use</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li>The template's <code>shopify.registerWebhooks</code>/webhook route helpers — signature verification is handled for you, but you should know what it's checking</li>
                  <li><a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/bullmq" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BullMQ</a> (needs Redis) or a simple Postgres-backed job table for anything that shouldn't block the webhook response</li>
                  <li>Shopify CLI's webhook trigger command, for firing test events locally without waiting on a real store action</li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Step-by-step path</summary>
                <ol class="path">
                  <li>Register a simple webhook topic (e.g. <code>products/update</code>) and log the payload to confirm delivery end to end.</li>
                  <li>Read the HMAC verification code the template generates so "why is this request rejected" is never a mystery.</li>
                  <li>Implement the three mandatory compliance webhooks, even on a hobby app — they're required to be handled, and it's good practice to see them.</li>
                  <li>Add a one-time recurring charge in test/dev-store billing mode and confirm the approval flow in the Admin.</li>
                </ol>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Free resources</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://shopify.dev/docs/apps/build/webhooks" target="_blank" rel="noopener">shopify.dev — Webhooks</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://shopify.dev/docs/apps/build/privacy-law-compliance" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Privacy/compliance webhooks</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://shopify.dev/docs/apps/launch/billing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Billing API docs</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Where people get lost</summary>
                <ul class="pitfalls">
                  <li><b>Webhooks can arrive more than once</b> — handlers must be idempotent (safe to run twice on the same event), not assume exactly-once delivery.</li>
                  <li><b>Doing slow work inside the handler</b> — Shopify expects a fast response; anything slow should be queued and processed after you've already returned 200.</li>
                  <li><b>Forgetting test mode for billing</b> — real charges on a dev store during development is an easy, avoidable mistake; always confirm <code>test: true</code> while building.</li>
                </ul>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Checkpoint project</summary>
                <p class="checkpoint">Register and verify one real webhook end to end, implement the mandatory compliance webhooks, and create one test-mode recurring charge.</p>
              </details>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="node" id="s-ext">
            <div class="card">
              <h3>Extensions <span class="badge">optional / advanced</span></h3>
              <p class="tagline">How an app reaches into the storefront and checkout, beyond the embedded admin page.</p>
              <ul>
                <li>Theme App Extensions — app blocks merchants add to their theme via the theme editor, no theme code edits needed</li>
                <li>UI Extensions — small React-like components injected into specific Shopify surfaces (checkout, customer account, admin blocks)</li>
                <li>Each extension type has its own sandboxed runtime and its own limited API surface — don't assume main-app code just drops in</li>
              </ul>

              <details>
                <summary>Tools you'll actually use</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li>Shopify CLI's extension generators (<code>shopify app generate extension</code>)</li>
                  <li><code>@shopify/ui-extensions-react</code> for checkout/customer-account UI extensions</li>
                  <li>A development store with a checkout you can actually test on (test payment gateway, no real charges)</li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Step-by-step path</summary>
                <ol class="path">
                  <li>Generate a Theme App Extension and add it as an app block in the theme editor on your dev store.</li>
                  <li>Generate a simple checkout UI extension and render static content on the test checkout.</li>
                  <li>Wire that extension to read data from your app's own backend via a network call.</li>
                </ol>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Free resources</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://shopify.dev/docs/apps/build/online-store/theme-app-extensions" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Theme app extensions</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://shopify.dev/docs/api/checkout-ui-extensions" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Checkout UI Extensions API</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Where people get lost</summary>
                <ul class="pitfalls">
                  <li><b>Assuming Polaris/App Bridge code works inside extensions</b> — extensions render in a separate sandboxed context with their own component library, not your admin app's React tree.</li>
                  <li><b>Underestimating review requirements</b> — checkout extensions go through extra scrutiny (performance budgets, allowed APIs) beyond a normal admin app.</li>
                </ul>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Checkpoint project</summary>
                <p class="checkpoint">Ship one Theme App Extension block visible on a dev store's storefront, treated as a stretch goal after the core app works.</p>
              </details>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="node" id="s-test">
            <div class="card">
              <h3>Testing a Shopify App</h3>
              <p class="tagline">Enough to trust a deploy — not full coverage from day one.</p>
              <ul>
                <li>Unit tests for pure logic (pricing math, formatting) — fast, no server needed</li>
                <li>Testing loaders/actions in isolation by calling them directly with a mocked request</li>
                <li>End-to-end smoke test: app installs, loads, and one core action succeeds, on a real dev store</li>
              </ul>

              <details>
                <summary>Tools you'll actually use</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://vitest.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vitest</a> — fast, works naturally with Vite-based projects like the React Router template</li>
                  <li><a href="https://testing-library.com/docs/react-testing-library/intro/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">React Testing Library</a> — for component behavior, not implementation details</li>
                  <li><a href="https://playwright.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Playwright</a> — for the occasional full end-to-end install/click-through test</li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Step-by-step path</summary>
                <ol class="path">
                  <li>Add Vitest and write one unit test for a pure function you already have.</li>
                  <li>Write one test that calls a loader directly with a fake request and asserts on its returned data shape.</li>
                  <li>Write one Playwright test that installs the app on a dev store and asserts the main page renders.</li>
                </ol>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Free resources</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://vitest.dev/guide/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vitest — Getting Started</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://playwright.dev/docs/intro" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Playwright — Getting Started</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Where people get lost</summary>
                <ul class="pitfalls">
                  <li><b>Trying to test everything through the browser</b> — most loader/action logic is plain functions and is far faster and more reliable to test directly than through a full UI click-through.</li>
                  <li><b>No tests at all until "later"</b> — even 3–4 tests around your riskiest action (the one that mutates real data) catches most regressions.</li>
                </ul>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Checkpoint project</summary>
                <p class="checkpoint">One passing unit test, one passing loader/action test, and one passing Playwright smoke test, wired into a script you can run with one command.</p>
              </details>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="node" id="s-deploy">
            <div class="card">
              <h3>DevOps / Deployment</h3>
              <p class="tagline">Shipping a real, installable app — for free.</p>
              <ul>
                <li>Env vars and secrets in production, kept out of source control</li>
                <li>Free deployment target: Vercel (Hobby, serverless) or Render (free web service, spins down when idle)</li>
                <li>Production database on Neon or Supabase, swapped in via <code>DATABASE_URL</code></li>
                <li>Production OAuth callback URLs, webhook reliability, and updating the app's URLs in the Partner dashboard</li>
              </ul>

              <details>
                <summary>Tools you'll actually use</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li>Vercel CLI or Render's dashboard/GitHub integration for deploys</li>
                  <li><code>shopify app deploy</code> — pushes your app's configuration (URLs, scopes, extensions) to Shopify, separate from deploying your actual server</li>
                  <li>A GitHub repo connected to your host, so every push to main redeploys automatically</li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Step-by-step path</summary>
                <ol class="path">
                  <li>Push the app to a GitHub repo.</li>
                  <li>Connect the repo to Vercel or Render, set env vars (including <code>DATABASE_URL</code> pointing at Neon/Supabase) in the host's dashboard, and deploy.</li>
                  <li>Update the app's URLs and redirect URLs in the Partner dashboard (or via <code>shopify.app.toml</code> + <code>shopify app deploy</code>) to point at the new production URL.</li>
                  <li>Run migrations against the production database and reinstall the app on the dev store using the production URL.</li>
                  <li>Trigger a real webhook and confirm it's received in production, not just locally.</li>
                </ol>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Free resources</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://shopify.dev/docs/apps/launch/deployment" target="_blank" rel="noopener">shopify.dev — Deployment docs</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://vercel.com/docs/frameworks/react-router" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vercel — Deploying React Router</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://render.com/docs/free" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Render — Free tier docs</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Where people get lost</summary>
                <ul class="pitfalls">
                  <li><b>"Works locally, 500s in production"</b> — almost always a missing env var or a database migration that was never run against the production database.</li>
                  <li><b>Forgetting the app config also needs deploying</b> — your server can be live while Shopify still points at old URLs/scopes, because those live in a separate config that needs its own <code>shopify app deploy</code>.</li>
                  <li><b>Free-tier cold starts mistaken for a bug</b> — a spun-down free Render service takes ~a minute to wake up; that first slow request is expected, not broken.</li>
                </ul>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Checkpoint project</summary>
                <p class="checkpoint">Deploy to a free host, connect a free production Postgres database, and install the app on a dev store end-to-end using the live URL — with webhooks and one billing charge confirmed working in production.</p>
              </details>
            </div>
          </div>

