          <div class="node" id="b-node">
            <div class="card">
              <h3>Node.js Fundamentals</h3>
              <p class="tagline">The runtime underneath everything else in this track.</p>
              <ul>
                <li>Event loop, modules (CommonJS vs. ESM), npm</li>
                <li>Async patterns: callbacks → promises → <code>async</code>/<code>await</code>, and why they're the same mechanism underneath</li>
                <li>Building a plain <code>http</code> server before reaching for a framework</li>
                <li>Express or Fastify basics — routing, middleware, error handling</li>
              </ul>

              <details>
                <summary>Tools you'll actually use</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://expressjs.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Express</a> — most widely used, huge ecosystem, easiest to find help for</li>
                  <li><a href="https://fastify.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fastify</a> — faster, schema-based validation built in, worth knowing once Express feels familiar</li>
                  <li><a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/dotenv" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dotenv</a> — loading env vars locally (see the Toolchain track)</li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Step-by-step path</summary>
                <ol class="path">
                  <li>Build a server using only Node's built-in <code>http</code> module — no framework — to see what a framework is actually saving you from.</li>
                  <li>Rebuild the same server in Express, comparing routing and middleware to the raw version.</li>
                  <li>Add middleware for logging and centralized error handling.</li>
                  <li>Build a small REST API (e.g. a task tracker) with a couple of routes and in-memory data — no database yet.</li>
                </ol>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Free resources</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://nodejs.org/en/learn/getting-started/introduction-to-nodejs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Node.js — Getting Started Guide</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://expressjs.com/en/starter/guide.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Express — Getting Started</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://nodejs.dev/en/learn/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">nodejs.dev — Learn</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Where people get lost</summary>
                <ul class="pitfalls">
                  <li><b>Forgetting to <code>await</code> a promise</b> — the function silently continues without the data you expected; look for a value that's a <code>Promise</code> object instead of the data itself.</li>
                  <li><b>Unhandled promise rejections</b> crashing the process in production — always wrap async route handlers so errors reach your error-handling middleware.</li>
                  <li><b>Blocking the event loop</b> with heavy synchronous work (large JSON parsing, crypto) — Node is single-threaded for your code; one slow synchronous call stalls every other request.</li>
                </ul>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Checkpoint project</summary>
                <p class="checkpoint">A small REST API (task tracker) with in-memory data, proper error-handling middleware, and no unhandled rejections under normal use.</p>
              </details>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="node" id="b-http">
            <div class="card">
              <h3>HTTP &amp; API Design</h3>
              <p class="tagline">The contract every client of your backend relies on.</p>
              <ul>
                <li>Methods and idempotency — <code>GET</code>/<code>PUT</code>/<code>DELETE</code> should be safely repeatable, <code>POST</code> generally isn't</li>
                <li>Status codes that actually mean something (400 vs. 401 vs. 403 vs. 404 vs. 422 vs. 500) instead of always returning 200 or always 500</li>
                <li>Request/response shape consistency, versioning, and pagination conventions</li>
                <li>REST vs. GraphQL — when a query language earns its complexity vs. when plain REST is simpler to build and debug</li>
              </ul>

              <details>
                <summary>Tools you'll actually use</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://www.postman.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Postman</a> or <a href="https://insomnia.rest/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Insomnia</a> — manually poking your own API before a frontend exists</li>
                  <li><a href="https://github.com/OAI/OpenAPI-Specification" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenAPI/Swagger</a> — documenting a REST API in a machine-readable, free, standard format</li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Step-by-step path</summary>
                <ol class="path">
                  <li>Take the task tracker API and audit every route's status codes against what they should actually return.</li>
                  <li>Add consistent error response shape (e.g. <code>{'{'} error: {'{'} message, code {'}'} {'}'}</code>) across all routes.</li>
                  <li>Write an OpenAPI spec for it by hand, then generate docs from it.</li>
                </ol>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Free resources</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN — HTTP</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://restfulapi.net/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RESTful API design guide</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Where people get lost</summary>
                <ul class="pitfalls">
                  <li><b>Everything returns 200</b> with an <code>{'{'} error: ... {'}'}</code> body — technically works, but breaks every HTTP client/monitoring tool that relies on status codes.</li>
                  <li><b>Using <code>GET</code> for anything that changes data</b> — breaks caching and can get triggered accidentally by prefetching or crawlers.</li>
                </ul>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Checkpoint project</summary>
                <p class="checkpoint">The task tracker API returns correct, consistent status codes and error shapes for every route, documented in a short OpenAPI spec.</p>
              </details>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="node" id="b-pg">
            <div class="card">
              <h3>PostgreSQL Fundamentals</h3>
              <p class="tagline">Relational modeling and SQL, written by hand before adding an ORM.</p>
              <ul>
                <li>Tables, relations, primary/foreign keys, normalization basics</li>
                <li>Core SQL: <code>SELECT</code>, <code>JOIN</code>, <code>GROUP BY</code>, indexes</li>
                <li>Writing and running migrations manually before letting a tool generate them</li>
                <li>Query performance basics — <code>EXPLAIN</code>, when an index actually helps</li>
              </ul>

              <details>
                <summary>Tools you'll actually use</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><code>psql</code> — the command-line client, worth being comfortable in even if you mostly use a GUI</li>
                  <li><a href="https://www.pgadmin.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">pgAdmin</a> or <a href="https://tableplus.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TablePlus</a> (free tier) — GUI for browsing tables and running queries</li>
                  <li>Docker (see below) to run Postgres locally without installing it system-wide</li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Step-by-step path</summary>
                <ol class="path">
                  <li>Run Postgres locally via Docker (<code>docker run postgres</code>) rather than a native install.</li>
                  <li>Design and create the task tracker's schema by hand in raw SQL.</li>
                  <li>Wire the Node API to it with raw <code>pg</code> queries — no ORM yet — to feel exactly what an ORM later automates.</li>
                  <li>Run <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code> on a slow query, add an index, and re-run it to see the difference.</li>
                </ol>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Free resources</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PostgreSQL — Official Tutorial</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://pgexercises.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PG Exercises</a> — interactive SQL practice <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Where people get lost</summary>
                <ul class="pitfalls">
                  <li><b>N+1 queries</b> — looping over rows and querying again for each one instead of a single <code>JOIN</code>; invisible with 5 rows, catastrophic with 5,000.</li>
                  <li><b>No indexes on foreign keys</b> — Postgres doesn't auto-index every foreign key; joins and lookups on them can be slow until you add one.</li>
                  <li><b>Confusing "it ran" with "it's correct"</b> — a <code>JOIN</code> without the right condition silently multiplies rows (a Cartesian product) instead of erroring.</li>
                </ul>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Checkpoint project</summary>
                <p class="checkpoint">The task tracker's schema and all queries are hand-written SQL against a Dockerized Postgres, with at least one index added after profiling a slow query.</p>
              </details>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="node" id="b-prisma">
            <div class="card">
              <h3>Prisma ORM</h3>
              <p class="tagline">Replacing hand-written SQL with a typed data layer, now that you know what it's automating.</p>
              <ul>
                <li>Prisma schema syntax and relations</li>
                <li>Migrations with Prisma Migrate</li>
                <li>Querying with Prisma Client — <code>include</code>/<code>select</code>, transactions</li>
                <li>When to drop to raw SQL anyway (complex reporting queries, bulk operations)</li>
              </ul>

              <details>
                <summary>Tools you'll actually use</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li>Prisma CLI and Prisma Studio (same tools as the Shopify track's Prisma module)</li>
                  <li><code>prisma.$transaction()</code> for multi-step writes that must succeed or fail together</li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Step-by-step path</summary>
                <ol class="path">
                  <li>Rebuild the task tracker's data layer on Prisma, migrating the existing raw-SQL schema over field by field.</li>
                  <li>Reproduce the earlier <code>JOIN</code>-based query using <code>include</code>, and compare the generated SQL (Prisma can log it) to what you wrote by hand.</li>
                  <li>Wrap a multi-step write (e.g. create a task and a log entry together) in a transaction.</li>
                </ol>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Free resources</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://www.prisma.io/docs/getting-started" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Prisma — Getting Started</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://www.prisma.io/docs/orm/prisma-client/queries/transactions" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Prisma transactions</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Where people get lost</summary>
                <ul class="pitfalls">
                  <li><b>Not knowing what SQL Prisma actually generates</b> — enable query logging early; an ORM that hides its SQL forever makes performance problems invisible until they hurt.</li>
                  <li><b>Editing the migration files by hand after they're applied</b> — Prisma tracks which migrations already ran; hand-editing an applied one desyncs your history. Create a new migration instead.</li>
                </ul>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Checkpoint project</summary>
                <p class="checkpoint">Task tracker fully on Prisma, with one transaction-wrapped multi-step write and query logging turned on at least once to confirm the generated SQL looks sane.</p>
              </details>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="node" id="b-auth">
            <div class="card">
              <h3>Authentication &amp; Security</h3>
              <p class="tagline">Enough to not ship an obviously exploitable API.</p>
              <ul>
                <li>Password hashing (never plain text, never fast hashes like MD5/SHA1 for passwords)</li>
                <li>Session-cookie auth vs. JWT — what each is actually good for, and that JWTs aren't automatically "more secure"</li>
                <li>Input validation at the boundary — never trust a request body's shape or types</li>
                <li>The handful of OWASP issues that matter most day to day: SQL/NoSQL injection (mostly solved by using Prisma/parameterized queries), broken access control, and secrets in source control</li>
              </ul>

              <details>
                <summary>Tools you'll actually use</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/bcrypt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">bcrypt</a> (or <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/argon2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">argon2</a>) for password hashing</li>
                  <li><a href="https://zod.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zod</a> — runtime schema validation for request bodies, paired naturally with TypeScript</li>
                  <li><a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/jsonwebtoken" target="_blank" rel="noopener">jsonwebtoken</a> if you do go the JWT route</li>
                  <li><a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/helmet" target="_blank" rel="noopener">helmet</a> — sane default HTTP security headers for Express</li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Step-by-step path</summary>
                <ol class="path">
                  <li>Add signup/login to the task tracker with bcrypt-hashed passwords and cookie-based sessions.</li>
                  <li>Add Zod schemas validating every request body, and return 400s with useful messages when validation fails.</li>
                  <li>Read through the OWASP Top 10 once, mapping each item to whether your app is currently exposed.</li>
                  <li>Scan your own repo for anything that looks like a committed secret, and move it to env vars.</li>
                </ol>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Free resources</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OWASP Top 10</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OWASP Cheat Sheet Series</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://zod.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zod documentation</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Where people get lost</summary>
                <ul class="pitfalls">
                  <li><b>Storing JWTs in <code>localStorage</code></b> — accessible to any injected script (XSS), unlike an <code>httpOnly</code> cookie. A common but risky default.</li>
                  <li><b>Trusting client-sent IDs</b> — e.g. a request body containing <code>userId</code> that the server just believes, instead of deriving the user from the authenticated session.</li>
                  <li><b>Validation only on the frontend</b> — a frontend form's validation is a UX nicety; the API must independently validate every request, since it can be called directly.</li>
                </ul>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Checkpoint project</summary>
                <p class="checkpoint">Task tracker has hashed-password signup/login, Zod-validated request bodies on every route, and no secrets committed to the repo.</p>
              </details>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="node" id="b-test">
            <div class="card">
              <h3>Testing</h3>
              <p class="tagline">The habit that makes refactoring safe instead of terrifying.</p>
              <ul>
                <li>Unit tests for pure business logic</li>
                <li>Integration tests hitting real routes against a real (test) database</li>
                <li>Test data setup/teardown so tests don't pollute each other</li>
              </ul>

              <details>
                <summary>Tools you'll actually use</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://vitest.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vitest</a> or <a href="https://jestjs.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jest</a></li>
                  <li><a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/supertest" target="_blank" rel="noopener">supertest</a> — hitting Express routes directly in tests without a real network call</li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Step-by-step path</summary>
                <ol class="path">
                  <li>Write unit tests for any pure functions (validation, formatting) in the task tracker.</li>
                  <li>Add supertest integration tests for the main routes against a disposable test database.</li>
                  <li>Add a script that resets the test database between runs so tests are repeatable.</li>
                </ol>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Free resources</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://vitest.dev/guide/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vitest — Getting Started</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://github.com/ladjs/supertest" target="_blank" rel="noopener">supertest — README/examples</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Where people get lost</summary>
                <ul class="pitfalls">
                  <li><b>Tests that depend on run order</b> — one test's leftover data silently makes another pass or fail. Reset state between tests, don't rely on execution sequence.</li>
                  <li><b>Mocking the database entirely</b> — fast, but misses real SQL/constraint bugs; a real (disposable) test database catches issues mocks can't.</li>
                </ul>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Checkpoint project</summary>
                <p class="checkpoint">A test suite covering the task tracker's core routes end to end against a disposable test database, runnable with one command and safe to run repeatedly.</p>
              </details>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="node" id="b-docker">
            <div class="card">
              <h3>Docker</h3>
              <p class="tagline">Containerizing the app for local/production parity.</p>
              <ul>
                <li>Images, containers, Dockerfile basics</li>
                <li><code>docker-compose</code> for app + Postgres locally, one command to start everything</li>
                <li>Volumes for persistent local data</li>
                <li>Multi-stage builds for smaller production images</li>
              </ul>

              <details>
                <summary>Tools you'll actually use</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li>Docker Desktop (free for personal use) or the Docker Engine CLI directly on Linux</li>
                  <li><code>docker-compose.yml</code> — one file describing your app + database + any other services together</li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Step-by-step path</summary>
                <ol class="path">
                  <li>Write a Dockerfile for the task tracker and build/run it standalone.</li>
                  <li>Add a <code>docker-compose.yml</code> that runs the app and Postgres together, sharing a network.</li>
                  <li>Add a named volume so Postgres data survives a container restart.</li>
                  <li>Convert the Dockerfile to a multi-stage build and compare image size before/after.</li>
                </ol>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Free resources</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://docs.docker.com/get-started/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Docker — Get Started</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://docs.docker.com/build/building/multi-stage/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Multi-stage builds</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Where people get lost</summary>
                <ul class="pitfalls">
                  <li><b>Forgetting a volume</b> — without one, Postgres data disappears every time the container is removed, which reads as "my database keeps getting wiped."</li>
                  <li><b>Copying <code>node_modules</code> into the image</b> — install dependencies inside the container instead; a <code>.dockerignore</code> should exclude the local <code>node_modules</code> and <code>.env</code>.</li>
                </ul>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Checkpoint project</summary>
                <p class="checkpoint">A Dockerfile and docker-compose.yml that run the task tracker and its Postgres database together locally with one command, data persisting across restarts.</p>
              </details>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="node" id="b-ci">
            <div class="card">
              <h3>GitHub Actions (CI/CD)</h3>
              <p class="tagline">Automating tests and builds on every push.</p>
              <ul>
                <li>Workflow syntax, triggers, jobs, steps</li>
                <li>Running lint/tests on every pull request</li>
                <li>Building and pushing a Docker image</li>
                <li>Secrets management in workflows</li>
              </ul>

              <details>
                <summary>Tools you'll actually use</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li>GitHub Actions — free for public repos, and free minutes included for private ones</li>
                  <li>GitHub Container Registry (or Docker Hub free tier) for pushing built images</li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Step-by-step path</summary>
                <ol class="path">
                  <li>Add a workflow that runs lint + the test suite on every push.</li>
                  <li>Make it required to pass before a PR can merge (branch protection).</li>
                  <li>Add a second job that builds and pushes the Docker image on merges to main.</li>
                  <li>Store the database URL/secrets in GitHub Actions secrets, never in the workflow file.</li>
                </ol>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Free resources</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://docs.github.com/en/actions/writing-workflows/quickstart" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub Actions — Quickstart</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://docs.github.com/en/actions/security-guides/using-secrets-in-github-actions" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Using secrets in GitHub Actions</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Where people get lost</summary>
                <ul class="pitfalls">
                  <li><b>Workflow "passes" locally but not in CI</b> — usually a missing env var or a dependency that only happened to be installed on your machine; CI should start from a clean checkout every time.</li>
                  <li><b>Secrets printed in logs</b> — never <code>echo</code> a secret directly; GitHub masks known secret values in logs but only if they're referenced through the secrets context correctly.</li>
                </ul>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Checkpoint project</summary>
                <p class="checkpoint">A workflow that lints, tests, and builds the Docker image on every push to main, with secrets handled correctly and merges blocked on a failing check.</p>
              </details>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="node" id="b-cache">
            <div class="card">
              <h3>Caching &amp; Performance</h3>
              <p class="tagline">The 20% that solves most real slowness — worth knowing even briefly.</p>
              <ul>
                <li>What's worth caching: expensive/repeated reads, not writes</li>
                <li>In-memory caching vs. Redis (shared across multiple server instances)</li>
                <li>Cache invalidation — the actually hard part; stale data is a worse bug than a slow request</li>
                <li>Basic HTTP caching headers (<code>Cache-Control</code>, <code>ETag</code>) for free wins on static/rarely-changing responses</li>
              </ul>

              <details>
                <summary>Tools you'll actually use</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://redis.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Redis</a> — run locally via Docker; <a href="https://upstash.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Upstash</a> offers a free serverless Redis tier for a deployed project</li>
                  <li>Node's built-in <code>Map</code> for the simplest possible in-process cache while learning</li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Step-by-step path</summary>
                <ol class="path">
                  <li>Find the slowest route in the task tracker and measure it before changing anything.</li>
                  <li>Add a simple in-memory cache for it with a short TTL, and measure again.</li>
                  <li>Swap the in-memory cache for Redis (via Docker locally) so it would survive a server restart/multiple instances.</li>
                  <li>Deliberately go stale — change the underlying data without invalidating the cache — to feel why invalidation is the hard part.</li>
                </ol>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Free resources</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://redis.io/docs/latest/develop/get-started/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Redis — Get Started</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Caching" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN — HTTP caching</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Where people get lost</summary>
                <ul class="pitfalls">
                  <li><b>Caching before measuring</b> — adds complexity and stale-data bugs to a route that wasn't actually slow. Profile first.</li>
                  <li><b>No expiry at all</b> — a cache with no TTL and no invalidation path is just a second, hidden source of truth waiting to disagree with the database.</li>
                </ul>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Checkpoint project</summary>
                <p class="checkpoint">One measurably slow route, cached with Redis and a sensible TTL, with a before/after timing comparison you can show.</p>
              </details>
            </div>
          </div>

