          <div class="node" id="t-pkg">
            <div class="card">
              <h3>package.json &amp; Package Managers</h3>
              <p class="tagline">The first file to read in any Node project — it tells you how to run it.</p>
              <ul>
                <li><code>scripts</code> — the actual commands the project runs (<code>dev</code>, <code>build</code>, <code>start</code>, <code>test</code>); always check these before guessing a command</li>
                <li><code>dependencies</code> vs. <code>devDependencies</code> — what ships to production vs. what's only needed to build/test</li>
                <li>Semver ranges (<code>^1.2.3</code> vs. <code>~1.2.3</code> vs. exact) and why the lockfile, not this file, decides the exact installed version</li>
                <li>Workspaces/monorepos — one repo, multiple <code>package.json</code>s, shared dependencies hoisted to a root <code>node_modules</code></li>
              </ul>

              <details>
                <summary>Tools you'll actually use</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li>npm (ships with Node, default everywhere) — or pnpm, faster and stricter about not letting you import undeclared dependencies</li>
                  <li>Lockfiles: <code>package-lock.json</code> (npm), <code>pnpm-lock.yaml</code>, <code>yarn.lock</code> — always commit these, never hand-edit them</li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Step-by-step path</summary>
                <ol class="path">
                  <li>Open any project you've cloned and read <code>scripts</code> before touching anything else — this is how you find out how to actually run it.</li>
                  <li>Run <code>npm ls &lt;package&gt;</code> to see exactly which version is installed and why (which dependency pulled it in).</li>
                  <li>Deliberately bump a dependency's version in <code>package.json</code>, reinstall, and watch the lockfile change.</li>
                  <li>Try turning a two-app project into an npm workspaces monorepo and see what moves to the root.</li>
                </ol>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Free resources</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://docs.npmjs.com/cli/v10/using-npm/scripts" target="_blank" rel="noopener">npm docs — scripts</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://docs.npmjs.com/about-semantic-versioning" target="_blank" rel="noopener">npm docs — semantic versioning</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://docs.npmjs.com/cli/v10/using-npm/workspaces" target="_blank" rel="noopener">npm docs — workspaces</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Where people get lost</summary>
                <ul class="pitfalls">
                  <li><b>Assuming <code>npm start</code> always works</b> — many projects use a different script name (<code>dev</code>, <code>serve</code>); always check <code>scripts</code> first.</li>
                  <li><b>Deleting the lockfile "to fix" an install error</b> — it can silently upgrade every dependency at once and break something unrelated; try <code>rm -rf node_modules &amp;&amp; npm install</code> first, keeping the lockfile.</li>
                  <li><b>Mixing package managers in one project</b> (npm install in a pnpm project) — creates two conflicting lockfiles; stick to whichever the repo already uses.</li>
                </ul>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Checkpoint project</summary>
                <p class="checkpoint">Given an unfamiliar cloned repo, correctly identify — without running anything — how to install, run in dev mode, and build it, purely by reading <code>package.json</code>.</p>
              </details>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="node" id="t-tsconfig">
            <div class="card">
              <h3>tsconfig.json &amp; the TypeScript Compiler</h3>
              <p class="tagline">Configures how strictly and how the project is type-checked and compiled.</p>
              <ul>
                <li><code>strict</code> — turns on the group of checks that catch the most real bugs (null checks especially); most serious projects run with it on</li>
                <li><code>target</code>/<code>module</code> — what JS syntax/module format the compiler outputs, tied to where the code will run</li>
                <li><code>paths</code>/<code>baseUrl</code> — import aliases like <code>~/components</code> instead of long relative paths</li>
                <li><code>include</code>/<code>exclude</code> — which files are even part of the TypeScript project</li>
              </ul>

              <details>
                <summary>Tools you'll actually use</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><code>tsc --noEmit</code> — type-check without producing output files, the command CI usually runs</li>
                  <li>VS Code's TypeScript status bar (bottom right) — shows which <code>tsconfig.json</code> and TS version is actually active</li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Step-by-step path</summary>
                <ol class="path">
                  <li>Open a project's <code>tsconfig.json</code> and identify <code>strict</code>, <code>target</code>, and any <code>paths</code> aliases before reading further code.</li>
                  <li>Run <code>tsc --noEmit</code> in a project and read every error it reports, even ones you don't plan to fix yet.</li>
                  <li>Toggle <code>strict</code> off in a small project and see how many previously-caught bugs go silent.</li>
                  <li>Add a path alias and confirm both the compiler and your bundler (see Build Tools) agree on it — a common source of "works in the editor, fails at build" errors.</li>
                </ol>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Free resources</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://www.typescriptlang.org/tsconfig" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TypeScript — tsconfig reference</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://www.totaltypescript.com/tsconfig-cheat-sheet" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Total TypeScript — tsconfig cheat sheet</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Where people get lost</summary>
                <ul class="pitfalls">
                  <li><b>Editor shows no errors, build fails anyway</b> — the editor may be using a different/cached TypeScript version than the one in the project's <code>node_modules</code>; check VS Code's TS version selector.</li>
                  <li><b>Path aliases work in the editor but not at runtime</b> — <code>tsconfig.json</code> paths are a compile-time hint only; the actual bundler/runtime needs its own matching alias config.</li>
                </ul>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Checkpoint project</summary>
                <p class="checkpoint">Explain, for a real project's <code>tsconfig.json</code>, what each top-level option does and what would visibly change if it were removed.</p>
              </details>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="node" id="t-jsdoc">
            <div class="card">
              <h3>JSDoc — Types Without TypeScript</h3>
              <p class="tagline">Some repos (and libraries) use plain JavaScript with JSDoc comments for types instead of <code>.ts</code> files — same benefit, different syntax.</p>
              <ul>
                <li><code>@param</code>/<code>@returns</code>/<code>@type</code> — the annotations that give a plain <code>.js</code> file the same editor autocomplete and checking as TypeScript</li>
                <li><code>@typedef</code> — defining a reusable shape, the JSDoc equivalent of a TS <code>type</code>/<code>interface</code></li>
                <li>How editors and <code>tsc</code> read these comments to type-check <code>.js</code> files with <code>checkJs</code> turned on, without any build step</li>
                <li>Why a library ships JSDoc-based types (<code>.d.ts</code> files) even if you never see a line of TypeScript source in it</li>
              </ul>

              <details>
                <summary>Tools you'll actually use</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><code>// @ts-check</code> at the top of a <code>.js</code> file, or <code>"checkJs": true</code> in <code>tsconfig.json</code> — turns on type-checking for plain JS</li>
                  <li>VS Code — reads JSDoc automatically, no config needed, which is why hovering a JS function from a library still shows you its types</li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Step-by-step path</summary>
                <ol class="path">
                  <li>Take one small <code>.ts</code> function you've already written and rewrite it as plain <code>.js</code> with equivalent JSDoc annotations.</li>
                  <li>Add <code>// @ts-check</code> to that file and confirm you still get the same red squiggles for a wrong argument type.</li>
                  <li>Open a popular library's compiled output in <code>node_modules</code> and find its <code>.d.ts</code> file, to see the "compiled" form of what JSDoc/TS source produces.</li>
                </ol>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Free resources</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://jsdoc.app/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">jsdoc.app — official tag reference</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/jsdoc-supported-types.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TypeScript Handbook — JSDoc-supported types</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Where people get lost</summary>
                <ul class="pitfalls">
                  <li><b>Assuming JSDoc comments are "just documentation"</b> and skimming past them — in a <code>checkJs</code> project they're doing the exact same job a <code>.ts</code> file's type annotations would.</li>
                  <li><b>Not realizing a <code>.js</code> file is type-checked at all</b> — if there's a <code>tsconfig.json</code> with <code>checkJs: true</code>, plain-looking JS files are still subject to type errors; missing this makes an error message feel like it's coming from nowhere.</li>
                </ul>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Checkpoint project</summary>
                <p class="checkpoint">Convert one small module between JSDoc-annotated <code>.js</code> and equivalent <code>.ts</code> in both directions, confirming the same errors are caught either way.</p>
              </details>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="node" id="t-build">
            <div class="card">
              <h3>Build Tools &amp; Bundlers</h3>
              <p class="tagline">What actually happens when you run <code>npm run dev</code> or <code>npm run build</code>.</p>
              <ul>
                <li>What a bundler does: follows your <code>import</code> graph, combines files, transforms TS/JSX into plain JS, optimizes for the browser</li>
                <li>Dev server vs. production build — dev prioritizes fast rebuilds (HMR: hot module replacement) over output size; build prioritizes small, cacheable output</li>
                <li>Vite — the default for new React/React Router projects today; config lives in <code>vite.config.ts</code></li>
                <li>Why a "build tool config file" is just a plain object telling the tool which plugins to use and where things live — not fundamentally different from any other config file once you know the shape</li>
              </ul>

              <details>
                <summary>Tools you'll actually use</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://vite.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vite</a> — used by the Shopify React Router template and most modern frontend projects</li>
                  <li><a href="https://esbuild.github.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">esbuild</a> — the very fast bundler Vite uses under the hood for dev; worth knowing it's there even if you never configure it directly</li>
                  <li>Webpack — older, still common in existing large projects; concepts transfer even if the config syntax looks different</li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Step-by-step path</summary>
                <ol class="path">
                  <li>Run <code>npm run build</code> on a Vite project and open the output folder — look at what actually got produced vs. your source files.</li>
                  <li>Open <code>vite.config.ts</code> in that project and identify every plugin listed, looking each one up if unfamiliar.</li>
                  <li>Add a path alias in <code>vite.config.ts</code> and confirm it matches the one you set in <code>tsconfig.json</code> — see how the two configs have to agree.</li>
                  <li>Intentionally break an import and read the bundler's error — it usually names the exact file and line that couldn't be resolved.</li>
                </ol>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Free resources</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://vite.dev/guide/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vite — Getting Started</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://vite.dev/config/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vite — Config reference</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Where people get lost</summary>
                <ul class="pitfalls">
                  <li><b>Treating the config file as unreadable magic</b> — it's a plain JS/TS object; read it top to bottom like any other file, looking up unfamiliar plugin names one at a time instead of all at once.</li>
                  <li><b>Dev-mode-only behavior mistaken for a bug</b> — some things (certain env var handling, source maps) differ between dev and a production build; always retest a suspected bug against a real build before assuming it's fixed or broken.</li>
                  <li><b>Import path works locally, fails in CI/build</b> — usually case sensitivity (macOS is case-insensitive by default, Linux CI isn't) or a missing alias in the bundler config.</li>
                </ul>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Checkpoint project</summary>
                <p class="checkpoint">Given any unfamiliar <code>vite.config.ts</code>, list every plugin and alias it configures and explain what each one is for.</p>
              </details>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="node" id="t-lint">
            <div class="card">
              <h3>ESLint, Prettier &amp; Git Hygiene</h3>
              <p class="tagline">The quiet infrastructure that keeps a codebase (and its history) consistent.</p>
              <ul>
                <li>ESLint — catches likely bugs and enforces code patterns (rules), configured via <code>eslint.config.js</code> (flat config) or the older <code>.eslintrc</code></li>
                <li>Prettier — pure formatting (whitespace, quotes, line length), deliberately has almost no configurable rules by design</li>
                <li><code>.gitignore</code> — what never gets committed (<code>node_modules</code>, build output, <code>.env</code>)</li>
                <li>Pre-commit hooks (Husky + lint-staged) — running lint/format automatically before a commit is even allowed</li>
              </ul>

              <details>
                <summary>Tools you'll actually use</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://eslint.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ESLint</a> with <code>typescript-eslint</code> for TS-aware rules</li>
                  <li><a href="https://prettier.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Prettier</a></li>
                  <li><a href="https://typicode.github.io/husky/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Husky</a> + <a href="https://github.com/okonet/lint-staged" target="_blank" rel="noopener">lint-staged</a> — only lint/format the files you actually changed, not the whole repo, on every commit</li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Step-by-step path</summary>
                <ol class="path">
                  <li>Add ESLint + Prettier to a project from scratch and reconcile any rules that conflict between them.</li>
                  <li>Run the linter and fix every warning by hand once, so you've seen what each rule is actually catching.</li>
                  <li>Add Husky + lint-staged so a bad commit is caught locally instead of in CI.</li>
                  <li>Write a proper <code>.gitignore</code> and confirm <code>git status</code> is clean after a fresh <code>npm install</code>.</li>
                </ol>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Free resources</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://eslint.org/docs/latest/use/getting-started" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ESLint — Getting Started</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://prettier.io/docs/en/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Prettier docs</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://github.com/github/gitignore" target="_blank" rel="noopener">github/gitignore — template collection</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Where people get lost</summary>
                <ul class="pitfalls">
                  <li><b>ESLint and Prettier fighting each other</b> — formatting-related ESLint rules conflicting with Prettier's own formatting; use <code>eslint-config-prettier</code> to turn off the overlapping ESLint rules rather than fighting both.</li>
                  <li><b>Accidentally committing <code>.env</code></b> because <code>.gitignore</code> was added after the first commit — once committed, it stays in history even after deleting it later; needs to be removed from history entirely if it held a real secret.</li>
                </ul>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Checkpoint project</summary>
                <p class="checkpoint">A small project with ESLint, Prettier (non-conflicting), and a Husky pre-commit hook that blocks a badly formatted or lint-failing commit.</p>
              </details>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="node" id="t-prisma">
            <div class="card">
              <h3>Reading Prisma Files</h3>
              <p class="tagline">A Prisma project always has the same handful of files — once you know what each does, none of them are mysterious.</p>
              <ul>
                <li><code>schema.prisma</code> — the single source of truth: datasource (which database), generator (what to generate, usually the client), and your models</li>
                <li><code>prisma/migrations/</code> — one timestamped folder per migration, each with the raw SQL that was actually run</li>
                <li>The generated Prisma Client (<code>node_modules/.prisma/client</code> or a custom output path) — code you never hand-edit, regenerated from the schema</li>
                <li><code>prisma/seed.ts</code> (if present) — a script to populate a fresh database with starter data, usually wired to run automatically after <code>migrate reset</code></li>
              </ul>

              <details>
                <summary>Tools you'll actually use</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li>Prisma CLI — <code>migrate dev</code>, <code>migrate deploy</code> (production), <code>studio</code>, <code>generate</code>, <code>db seed</code></li>
                  <li>Prisma VS Code extension — schema syntax highlighting, formatting, and jump-to-definition for models</li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Step-by-step path</summary>
                <ol class="path">
                  <li>Open any project's <code>schema.prisma</code> and identify every model, its fields, and its relations before looking at any application code.</li>
                  <li>Open the most recent folder under <code>prisma/migrations/</code> and read the raw SQL — confirm it matches what you'd expect from the schema.</li>
                  <li>Run <code>migrate dev</code> after a schema change and watch a new migration folder appear.</li>
                  <li>Write (or read, if one exists) a <code>seed.ts</code> and run <code>db seed</code> against a freshly reset database.</li>
                </ol>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Free resources</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://www.prisma.io/docs/orm/prisma-schema/data-model/models" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Prisma — data model reference</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://www.prisma.io/docs/orm/prisma-migrate/workflows/seeding" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Prisma — seeding</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Where people get lost</summary>
                <ul class="pitfalls">
                  <li><b>"Property does not exist on type" right after adding a model</b> — almost always a forgotten <code>prisma generate</code>; the client's types are generated, not inferred live.</li>
                  <li><b>Editing an already-applied migration file</b> — Prisma tracks applied migrations by name/checksum; editing history desyncs it. Create a new migration instead.</li>
                  <li><b><code>migrate dev</code> in production</b> — it's interactive and can prompt to reset data; production deploys should use <code>migrate deploy</code> instead.</li>
                </ul>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Checkpoint project</summary>
                <p class="checkpoint">Given an unfamiliar project, read <code>schema.prisma</code> and correctly diagram the models and their relations on paper before opening any application code.</p>
              </details>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="node" id="t-env">
            <div class="card">
              <h3>Environment Variables &amp; Config Across Environments</h3>
              <p class="tagline">Why the same code behaves differently on your machine, in CI, and in production.</p>
              <ul>
                <li><code>.env</code> (local, gitignored, real values) vs. <code>.env.example</code> (committed, documents what's needed with placeholder values)</li>
                <li>How env vars actually reach the process — loaded by a library like <code>dotenv</code> locally, injected directly by the host (Render/Vercel dashboard) in production</li>
                <li>Typing/validating <code>process.env</code> so a missing var fails loudly at startup instead of causing a confusing runtime error later</li>
                <li>Per-environment config (dev/staging/production) — different database URLs, different Shopify app URLs, without changing code</li>
              </ul>

              <details>
                <summary>Tools you'll actually use</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/dotenv" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dotenv</a> — loads <code>.env</code> into <code>process.env</code> locally</li>
                  <li>Zod (again) for validating <code>process.env</code> against a schema at startup</li>
                  <li>Your host's dashboard (Render/Vercel/Neon) for setting the real production values — never committed anywhere</li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Step-by-step path</summary>
                <ol class="path">
                  <li>Create a <code>.env.example</code> for a project listing every required variable with a placeholder, and confirm a fresh clone + copy-to-<code>.env</code> is enough to run it.</li>
                  <li>Write a small Zod schema that validates <code>process.env</code> at startup and throws a clear error naming the missing variable.</li>
                  <li>Deliberately deploy without setting one required env var on your host and read the resulting error, so you recognize it next time.</li>
                </ol>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Free resources</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/dotenv" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dotenv — README</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://12factor.net/config" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Twelve-Factor App — Config</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Where people get lost</summary>
                <ul class="pitfalls">
                  <li><b>"Works on my machine"</b> is very often just a <code>.env</code> value that exists locally but was never set on the host.</li>
                  <li><b>Committing a real <code>.env</code></b> by accident — always verify it's in <code>.gitignore</code> before the first commit, not after.</li>
                  <li><b>Silent fallback values masking a missing var</b> — a default like <code>process.env.API_KEY || ''</code> hides the real problem until something fails much later and further from the cause.</li>
                </ul>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Checkpoint project</summary>
                <p class="checkpoint">A project with a complete <code>.env.example</code>, startup-time validation of every required env var, and a documented list of which values differ between local/dev-store and production.</p>
              </details>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="node" id="t-debug">
            <div class="card">
              <h3>Debugging Like a Pro</h3>
              <p class="tagline">The actual day-to-day skill — more valuable than knowing any single tool.</p>
              <ul>
                <li>Reading a stack trace bottom-to-top vs. top-to-bottom, and finding the first line that's actually your code (not a library's internals)</li>
                <li>Binary-searching a bug — comment out/isolate half the suspect code, narrow down, repeat</li>
                <li>Server-side vs. client-side logs — remembering that a loader/action's <code>console.log</code> shows up in your terminal, not the browser</li>
                <li>Reproducing a bug with the smallest possible example before trying to fix it in the full app</li>
              </ul>

              <details>
                <summary>Tools you'll actually use</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li>Browser DevTools — Console, Network tab (inspect real request/response payloads and headers), and the Sources tab for breakpoints in client code</li>
                  <li>VS Code's built-in debugger — set real breakpoints in Node code instead of littering it with <code>console.log</code></li>
                  <li>Prisma Studio and GraphiQL — for confirming what's actually in the database/API before assuming your application code is wrong</li>
                  <li>Postman/Insomnia — isolate whether a bug is in the API itself or in how the frontend calls it</li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Step-by-step path</summary>
                <ol class="path">
                  <li>Set up VS Code's Node debugger for one project and step through a request handler line by line, watching variables change.</li>
                  <li>Practice reading Network tab request/response pairs for a broken form submission — payload, status code, response body, in that order.</li>
                  <li>Take a real bug and write down, before touching code, what you'd expect to see if each of your top 2–3 hypotheses were true — then go check.</li>
                  <li>Reproduce a bug in the smallest file you can (a few lines), separate from the full app, before attempting a fix.</li>
                </ol>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Free resources</summary>
                <div class="res-list"><ul>
                  <li><a href="https://developer.chrome.com/docs/devtools/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chrome DevTools docs</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                  <li><a href="https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/nodejs/nodejs-debugging" target="_blank" rel="noopener">VS Code — Node.js debugging</a> <span class="free-tag">free</span></li>
                </ul></div>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Where people get lost</summary>
                <ul class="pitfalls">
                  <li><b>Changing multiple things at once while debugging</b> — makes it impossible to know which change actually fixed (or caused) the behavior. Change one thing, observe, repeat.</li>
                  <li><b>Reading only the top line of a stack trace</b> — the actual bug is often several frames down, at the last line that's your own code rather than a library's.</li>
                  <li><b>Assuming instead of checking</b> — "the API must be returning the wrong data" without actually looking at the Network tab response; check first, theorize second.</li>
                </ul>
              </details>

              <details>
                <summary>Checkpoint project</summary>
                <p class="checkpoint">Pick a real bug (yours or an open-source issue) and debug it using breakpoints and the Network tab only — no scattered <code>console.log</code> statements — writing down each hypothesis before checking it.</p>
              </details>
            </div>
